# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Poor design of function rebalanceToWeights() in SafEth.sol could result in loss of protocol funds through a ‘donation’ attack.

```jsx
// SafEth.sol
function rebalanceToWeights() external onlyOwner {
        uint256 ethAmountBefore = address(this).balance;
        for (uint i = 0; i < derivativeCount; i++) {
            if (derivatives[i].balance() > 0)
                derivatives[i].withdraw(derivatives[i].balance());
        }
        uint256 ethAmountAfter = address(this).balance;
        uint256 ethAmountToRebalance = ethAmountAfter - ethAmountBefore;
```

The line `uint256 ethAmountBefore = address(this).balance;` is both redundant and dangerous. 
It is redundant because the contract is not expected to hold ETH.  Just checking for `address(this).balance` after withdrawing derivatives is enough to know how much ETH to rebalance. 

It is dangerous as if there is ETH in `ethAmountBefore` , the protocol will only re-deposit `uint256 ethAmountToRebalance = ethAmountAfter - ethAmountBefore` , leaving more ETH in the contract. There is no way to withdraw ETH stuck in the contract

## Proof of Concept
-1. Protocol holds approximately 100 ETH and owner wishes to rebalance
-2. Attacker observes owner calling `rebalanceToWeights()` tx in mempool
-3. Attacker front runs tx by sending 100 ETH into protocol using low-level `call`
-4. `rebalanceToWeights()` is executed (with 0 ETH) leaving 200 ETH stuck in contract (of which 100 ETH is protocol’s funds)

In a griefing attack described above, it could lead to total loss of protocol’s funds. In a more benign scenario, if there was ETH balance in the contract, the owner would not be able to call rebalanceToWeights() as each call will result in more ETH stuck in the contract

