# Vulnerability details

## Impact
If a user triggers a "reentrant" call to the contract during the burn function execution in the `withdraw()` function, could lead to a denial of service (DoS) attack.
By repeatedly calling the contract and stealing Ether from the contract before the burn function is completed.

## Proof of Concept
In the withdraw function, the burn function is called before sending Ether to `msg.sender`, If the burn function triggers a reentrant call to this contract, then the `msg.sender` will receive the Ether before the burn function finishes executing, which could lead to a DoS attack.

The code for the vulnerable withdraw function is:
```solidity
    function withdraw(uint256 amount) external onlyOwner {
        RocketTokenRETHInterface(rethAddress()).burn(amount);
        // solhint-disable-next-line
        (bool sent, ) = address(msg.sender).call{value: address(this).balance}(
            ""
        );
        require(sent, "Failed to send Ether");
    }
```
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-03-asymmetry/blob/44b5cd94ebedc187a08884a7f685e950e987261c/contracts/SafEth/derivatives/Reth.sol#L107-L114

In this function, the burn function of the `RocketTokenRETHInterface` contract is called before transferring the ETH to the `msg.sender`, If the burn function triggers a reentrant call to the Reth contract, then the `msg.sender` will receive the ETH before the burn function finishes executing, and we can tell this can lead to a DoS attack as the contract will run out of ETH.

